<%# One entry in the staff-side transcript. Unlike the guest's own view
    this renders internal notes too, so the boundary between "the guest
    read this" and "the guest cannot see this" is the single most important
    thing on the screen. It is drawn three ways at once — a distinct
    background and border, a lock glyph, and the literal sentence "Internal
    note — the guest cannot see this" — because getting it wrong means a
    receptionist's private remark gets typed into a guest's chat, and no
    one of those three signals survives every way a person reads a screen
    in a hurry.

    dir="auto" on the body: guest and staff may be writing in different
    scripts, so each entry resolves its own direction. %>
<div id="<%= dom_id(message) %>" data-message-id="<%= message.id %>"
     class="flex flex-col gap-1 <%= message.guest? ? "items-start" : "items-end" %>">
  <% if message.internal? %>
    <div class="w-full max-w-[85%] rounded-lg border-2 border-dashed border-purple-400 bg-purple-50 px-3 py-2">
      <%# Deliberately not `uppercase`: CSS text-transform would make the
          sentence a reader sees differ from the sentence in this file, so a
          test asserting on either one would stop describing the other. It
          is also a long sentence, and long sentences in all caps are read
          more slowly — the opposite of what a warning needs. %>
      <p class="mb-1 flex items-center gap-1.5 text-xs font-semibold tracking-wide text-purple-800">
        <span aria-hidden="true">🔒</span>
        <%= t(".internal_note_banner") %>
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-purple-950" dir="auto"><%= message.body %></p>
      <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-purple-700">
        <%= staff_message_author(message) %> ·
        <time datetime="<%= message.created_at.iso8601 %>"><%= staff_time(message.created_at).strftime("%d %b %H:%M") %></time>
      </p>
    </div>
  <% else %>
    <div class="flex max-w-[85%] flex-col gap-0.5 <%= message.guest? ? "items-start" : "items-end" %>">
      <span class="text-xs font-medium text-gray-500"><%= staff_message_author(message) %></span>
      <%# The receptionist reads in the hotel's own language, with the
          guest's actual words one tap away. An untranslated bubble says so
          rather than looking like an ordinary message a colleague can read. %>
      <div class="rounded-lg px-3 py-2 text-sm <%= staff_message_bubble_classes(message) %>">
        <%= render "shared/translated_body", message: message, reader_locale: Current.hotel.staff_locale,
              translate: !message.staff?,
              show_label: t(".see_original"), hide_label: t(".hide_original") %>
      </div>
      <time datetime="<%= message.created_at.iso8601 %>" class="px-1 text-xs text-gray-400">
        <%= staff_time(message.created_at).strftime("%d %b %H:%M") %>
      </time>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</div>
